NAME
       patterns-advanced - hybrid triadic patterns, pattern fusion, and
                           resonance-driven pattern selection in RTT

SYNOPSIS
       Advanced patterns extend the core triadic pattern system with:
           • hybrid patterns
           • pattern fusion
           • resonance-driven selection
           • adaptive pattern switching
           • multi-pattern coherence rules

DESCRIPTION
       Advanced triadic patterns describe how multiple base patterns combine,
       interact, or adapt under resonance-time dynamics. They allow systems
       to shift structure based on context, resonance gradients, lineage
       history, or schema constraints.

       Advanced patterns do not replace the core patterns. They orchestrate
       them.

       WRSADC validates all advanced pattern structures before dispatch.

HYBRID PATTERNS
       A hybrid pattern is a structured combination of two or more base
       triadic patterns. Hybrids allow systems to express complex workflows
       that cannot be captured by a single pattern.

       Examples:

           ladder ⊗ spiral
                  Progressive refinement with deepening resonance.

           weave ⊗ constellation
                  Interleaved multi-agent coordination.

           nested ⊗ cascade
                  Recursive stages with dependent transitions.

           mirror ⊗ expansion
                  Symmetric multi-phase elaboration.

       Hybrids must satisfy structural-schema rules.

PATTERN FUSION
       Pattern fusion merges two patterns into a single unified structure.
       Unlike hybrids, fused patterns behave as one pattern with blended
       structural and resonance properties.

       Examples:

           spiral + ladder → helix
                  A fused pattern combining ascent and deepening cycles.

           sequential + weave → braided-sequence
                  A fused pattern combining rhythmic flow and concurrency.

           expansion + nested → fractal-expansion
                  A fused pattern with self-similar triadic elaboration.

       Fusion requires:
           • resonance compatibility
           • schema alignment
           • lineage continuity

       Illegal fusions are rejected by WRSADC.

RESONANCE-DRIVEN PATTERN SELECTION
       Resonance-time determines which pattern is most appropriate for a
       given cycle. This is known as resonance-driven selection.

       Selection criteria include:
           • resonance amplitude
           • resonance gradient
           • coherence thresholds
           • lineage history
           • operator resonance signatures
           • schema constraints

       Example:
           Low resonance → sequential
           Rising resonance → spiral
           High resonance → ladder or expansion
           Resonance inversion → mirror
           Multi-agent resonance → constellation

       Pattern selection is dynamic and may change across cycles.

ADAPTIVE PATTERN SWITCHING
       Systems may switch patterns mid-execution based on resonance shifts.

       Example:
           Start: sequential
           Mid-cycle: resonance increases → switch to spiral
           Late-cycle: resonance stabilizes → switch to ladder

       WRSADC ensures:
           • safe transitions
           • lineage continuity
           • schema compliance

       Unsafe transitions are blocked.

MULTI-PATTERN COHERENCE
       When multiple patterns coexist, coherence rules ensure stability:

           • no contradictory resonance flows
           • no incompatible nesting depths
           • no infinite recursion
           • no pattern inversion loops
           • no schema violations

       Coherence is validated at dispatch time.

PATTERN LIFECYCLE
       1. Author defines pattern-schema.
       2. Hybrid or fused pattern is constructed.
       3. Pattern is added to a resonance package.
       4. WRSADC validates pattern structure.
       5. Pattern is selected or switched based on resonance.
       6. Triads execute inside the Engine.
       7. Lineage is updated.

NOTES
       Advanced patterns allow RTT systems to adapt, evolve, and express
       complex structural behavior without sacrificing coherence. They are
       the choreography of resonance-time.

